Current attribution hypothesis, not yet an optimization verdict: the dominant
cost is lifecycle handoff and wake topology rather than arithmetic inside an
authority critical section. Single-peer cheap traffic performs about 77 write
acquisitions per transaction and nearly one Ready work/effect batch per
transaction; four peers reduce that to about 15 writes per transaction and
2.12 transactions per Ready work batch. Dependency shapes inherently expose
one Ready work per accepted transaction and about 115-121 writes per submitted
pair. `AuthoritySignals::publish_mutation()` currently broadcasts through one
shared notification to heterogeneous waiters, and failed checkout probes also
take the unique authority write lock. Replacing the broadcast with
`notify_one` is not a valid local fix: the wrong capability class can consume
the hint and strand runnable work.

The complete compute, Ready, maintenance and effect wait/checkout topology has
now been audited far enough to bound the first design space. One resolver,
multiple verifiers, the Ready driver, maintenance driver, effect publisher and
service backpressure waiters consume the same global mutation broadcast. With
eight verifiers, one mutation can therefore cause roughly one resolver probe,
up to sixteen verifier probes, three maintenance probes, one publisher probe
and one Ready read. Successful checkout and settlement publish the same global
broadcast again. The profiling harness disables block assembly, so template
lanes did not cause the measured amplification. Develop's separate verify
queue uses a wake-one plus drain protocol, but that queue is a second mutable
authority and retains the old race and panic/catch failure surfaces; it is not
an acceptable design to restore.

